    passageB2: `<p>Growing old is an inevitable part of life, something we all face despite our best efforts to <strong>postpone</strong> it. While cosmetic procedures like surgery and hair dye can temporarily mask the signs of ageing, they can't stop the clock. Medical advancements have certainly helped people live longer, but the focus should be on maintaining a good <strong>quality of life</strong> as we age. The ageing process affects everyone differently; some remain healthy and independent well into old age, while others require support earlier.</p><p>One of the most challenging aspects of ageing is the potential decline in mental sharpness. It's common for older individuals to experience short-term memory loss, even while easily recalling distant memories. In some cases, this can <strong>progress to</strong> dementia, often in the form of Alzheimer's disease, a condition that gradually worsens over time. However, it's important to remember that this isn't the case for all older people.</p><p>Many senior citizens maintain their mental <strong>faculties</strong> and view retirement as a chance to enjoy newfound freedom. Those with good pensions may even find themselves financially comfortable, able to <strong>indulge in</strong> holidays and other luxuries. As a result, businesses and governments are increasingly aware of the economic and social influence of older people, often referred to as the "<strong>grey market</strong>".</p><p>Unfortunately, respect for older people isn't universal. While some cultures are known for their <strong>veneration</strong> of elders, in many societies, older people are <strong>relegated to</strong> a low status and treated accordingly. This type of discrimination, known as <strong>ageism</strong>, often leads to older individuals being unfairly labelled as <strong>frail</strong> and unproductive. Those who hold such views should remember that ageing is a universal experience and that they too will one day be in the same position. It is important to <strong>foster</strong> a society where older people are valued and respected for their wisdom and experience, not <strong>dismissed</strong> because of their age. We should strive to create inclusive environments where everyone can <strong>age with dignity</strong>. This includes ensuring access to appropriate healthcare, social support, and opportunities for continued learning and engagement. By <strong>challenging stereotypes</strong> and promoting intergenerational understanding, we can build a more equitable and supportive society for all.</p>`,
    translationB2: `<p>Qarish hayotning muqarrar bir qismidir, barchamiz uni <strong>kechiktirishga</strong> urinishlarimizga qaramay duch kelamiz. Kosmetik muolajalar, masalan, jarrohlik va soch bo'yoqlari qarish alomatlarini vaqtincha yashirishi mumkin, ammo ular vaqtni to'xtata olmaydi. Tibbiyot sohasidagi yutuqlar odamlarning uzoqroq yashashiga yordam berdi, lekin e'tibor qariganimiz sari yaxshi <strong>hayot sifatini</strong> saqlashga qaratilishi kerak. Qarish jarayoni hamma uchun har xil ta'sir qiladi; ba'zilar keksalik davrida ham sog'lom va mustaqil bo'lib qoladi, boshqalari esa erta yordamga muhtoj bo'ladi.</p><p>Qarishning eng qiyin jihatlaridan biri - aqliy o'tkirlikning pasayishi ehtimolidir. Keksa odamlar uchun yaqin xotirani yo'qotish, hatto uzoq xotiralarni osongina eslash odatiy holdir. Ba'zi hollarda, bu vaqt o'tishi bilan asta-sekin yomonlashadigan holat bo'lgan Altsgeymer kasalligi shaklida <strong>rivojlanishi</strong> mumkin. Biroq, buni barcha keksa odamlar boshdan kechirmasligini unutmaslik kerak.</p><p>Ko'pgina nafaqaxo'rlar o'zlarining aqliy <strong>qobiliyatlarini</strong> saqlab qoladilar va nafaqani yangi erkinlikdan bahramand bo'lish imkoniyati sifatida ko'rishadi. Yaxshi pensiya oladiganlar hatto o'zlarini moliyaviy jihatdan qulay his qilishlari mumkin, ta'til va boshqa dabdabalarga <strong>berilishga</strong> qodir. Natijada, biznes va hukumatlar keksa odamlarning iqtisodiy va ijtimoiy ta'siridan tobora ko'proq xabardor bo'lib bormoqda, ular ko'pincha "<strong>kulrang bozor</strong>" deb ataladi.</p><p>Afsuski, keksa odamlarga hurmat hamma joyda bir xil emas. Ba'zi madaniyatlar oqsoqollarni <strong>ulug'lashi</strong> bilan mashhur bo'lsa-da, ko'plab jamiyatlarda keksa odamlar past mavqega <strong>tushiriladi</strong> va shunga mos ravishda muomala qilinadi. <strong>Eyjizm</strong> deb nomlanuvchi kamsitishning bu turi ko'pincha keksa odamlarni adolatsiz ravishda <strong>zaif</strong> va samarasiz deb belgilanishiga olib keladi. Bunday qarashlarga ega bo'lganlar qarish universal tajriba ekanligini va bir kun kelib o'zlari ham xuddi shunday holatda bo'lishini eslashlari kerak. Keksa odamlar yoshi tufayli <strong>rad etilmasdan</strong>, donoligi va tajribasi uchun qadrlanadigan va hurmat qilinadigan jamiyatni <strong>rivojlantirish</strong> muhimdir. Biz hamma <strong>qadr-qimmat bilan qarishi</strong> mumkin bo'lgan inklyuziv muhitni yaratishga intilishimiz kerak. Bu tegishli tibbiy yordam, ijtimoiy qo'llab-quvvatlash, doimiy o'rganish va ishtirok etish imkoniyatlariga kirishni ta'minlashni o'z ichiga oladi. <strong>Stereotiplarga qarshi chiqish</strong> va avlodlararo tushunishni rivojlantirish orqali biz barcha uchun yanada adolatli va qo'llab-quvvatlovchi jamiyatni qura olamiz.</p>`,
